  Source:  New Statesman & Society, June 24, 1994 v7 n308 p37(1).
   Title:  Frank Zappa: The Negative Dialectics of Poodle Play.
           _(book reviews)
  Author:  Charles Shaar Murray
  People:  Watson, Ben


Full Text COPYRIGHT Statesman and Nation Publishing Company Ltd. (UK)
1994

The convention that musicians make records while critics review them
was not effectively challenged until 1965, when The Who's early
singles arrived supported by Pete Townshend's theoretical scaffolding.
Townshend's amphetamined sloganeering about Pop Art and
auto-destruction was only a five-minute argument, though; The Mothers
of Invention's double-album debut Freak Out!, released a year later,
was the full half-hour.

The barrage of manifestos, critiques, quips and rants encrusting Freak
Out!'s gatefold sleeve not only enabled the recording to review
itself. It also introduced Frank Zappa's self-contained,
self-mythologising world of cross-references, catch-phrases, radical
doubt and dubious sex jokes.

There was, according to Zappa, "a conscious control of thematic and
structural elements flowing through each album, live performance and
interview". This culminated in his mid-1970s assertion that the
conceptual continuity of the group's output macrostruture" was so
snakily abstruse that nobody other than himself was qualified to
review it at all. Until now, that is. "Somebody in that audience out
there", Zappa proclaimed in 1970, "knows what we're doing, and that
person is getting off on it beyond his/her wildest comprehensions."
Ben Watson, come on down!

Your average Zappaphile makes a Trekkie seem rational by comparison.
He (rarely she) will respond with Pavlovian alacrity to stray
references to dental floss, vacuum cleaners, gas-masks, vegetables,
zircon-encrusted tweezers, or anything to do with dogs in general and
poodles in particular.

However, this absurdist tour-de-force of meta-criticism--a 600-page,
200,000-word monstrosity riddled with feetnote and packaged with a
photo of a debonair poodle bearing a startling resemblance to Mr Ron
Wood of The Rolling Stones rhythm-and-blues combo--raises the stakes
to dizzying heights. It is the work of a man utterly,
snoopdoggy-doggedly dedicated to his subject, but though it contains a
modicum of biographical material, its primary function is to describe,
annotate and unravel each and every knot and wrinkle of Zappa's
30-year, 57-album "project/object".

Zappa was a polymath capable of functioning simultaneously as
avant-garde serious composer, improvisatory guitar hero, social
satirist, libertarian philosopher and populist gagster. He was also an
obsessive, and from the beginning his work was designed to attract
fellow obsessives, including the kind who would contemplate writing a
book claiming that "Apostrophe(') was Zappa's King Lear". And that's
just for starters.

"Poodle play", as Watson defines it, is the fine art of relating any
reference in the Zappa canon to anything else in the known universe.
Poodle play insists that the aforementioned album is somehow related
to Plato's Phaedo (which Zappa has never read) because the poodle in
question is named Fido. Demented? Not arf! Even the notoriously
phlegmatic Zappa was impressed, as Watson relates in the book's
bittersweet epilogue.

In October 1993, less than six weeks before Zappa's death from cancer,
Watson finally flew to LA to meet his hero and read him sections of
the meisterwerk: "When I finished my dissertation on reification,
Nazism and teeth Frank simply leaned over and shook me by the hand."

Watson is undoubtedly the most appreciative audience FZ ever had. He
adores Adorno, disagrees with Derrida but declines to deride him, pays
equal attention to Philip K Dick and Johnny "Guitar" Watson and
substantially misrepresents your humble servant on at least two
occasions.

Zappa's worldview was a mix-and-match combo platter of the progressive
and the reactionary. His formidable popularity in eastern Europe can
be partially attributed to the fact that some of his pettier lyrics
provide less of an obstacle for non-native speakers of English. As a
paid-up member of the Socialist Workers Party, Watson naturally lapses
i radical squirm mode when confronting Zappa's unconcealed distaste
for unions, feminists and male homosexuality.

However, he makes one first-rate political point: that Zappa's music
annihilates class-based categories of consumption. By juxtaposing and
superimposing high-art devices adapted from avant-garde concert music
with locker-room humour and driving rhythm-and-blues, he forces all
but the most eclectic listener to confront something unfamiliar.

Watson wrote much of the early drafts of this book under the not
inappropriate pseudonym "Out To Lunch". It is. Are you? Like a
dementia-seeking missile, it will pursue hard-core Zappaphiles
wherever they hide. However, they can console themselves with the
thought that, while they may be twisted enough to read a book like
this, at least they weren't twisted enough to write it. And maybe
they'll get off on it beyond their wildest comprehensions. Civilians,
on the other hand, will find it as utterly impenetrable as Lumpy
Gravy.

